(* ============================================================================
   17. A FIGURE MAY HOLD BLOCK CONTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-036].
      A `figure` carries block `children` and MAY carry a `caption` node. It MAY carry
      `kind`: `image`, `table`, `listing`, `equation`, or `other`. An absent
      kind makes no claim about its contents. An HTML importer may supply a
      table inside a figure even though Carve source captions the table
      directly. The nested table retains its own caption, and the figure's
      caption describes the outer wrapper (CARVE-P12-033).
      The fallback matrix row `figure.children[table]` records target behavior.

      A canonical writer emits the content it can spell and reports any lost
      figure wrapper, mixed contents, or caption distinction. It MUST NOT
      silently choose one child as `target` or discard the others.

      A table inside a figure keeps its own caption. On a canonical write,
      the table's caption takes the single source `^ ` slot; an outer caption
      that cannot be spelled is reported. A writer MUST NOT emit a second
      `^ ` line: after a caption it would become literal body prose. A figure
      group's table panel is a plain table child, not this wrapper. Other
      captionable hosts from Carve source retain their figure wrapper.

   18. A CITATION DEFINITION IS A NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-037].
      `type: "citation_definition"`, carrying:
        key       the citation key as the author wrote it, without the `@`
        namespace  `citation`, identifying the key space shared with uses
        children  the entry's inline content: what follows the `]: ` separator
                  and the optional metadata block, parsed as inline
        attrs     the leading `{author= year=}` metadata block, when the
                  definition carries one
      and its `pos` under PART 12 §4, like every other node.

      SHAPED AFTER CARVE-P12-029's LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION, NOT AFTER THE FOOTNOTE.
      That is the closer of the two analogues: a `footnote` holds BLOCKS,
      while `[@key]: {author= year=} entry` holds a metadata run plus one line
      of rendered text. So the entry is `children` of INLINE nodes, and the
      metadata lands in `attrs` -- the same two slots CARVE-P12-029 spends on a
      definition line's tail. Both definition types use `key` for the label,
      because `citation.key` names the same string
      at the use site, and PART 12 §3 makes field names spec surface: one construct
      identified two ways in one document is the collision PART 12 §1 exists to stop.

      NO RENDERED OUTPUT MOVES ON ANY TARGET. The node renders nothing where it
      sits; the entry's text renders in the references list the extension
      builds. Conformance is asserted at the AST level.

      TIER-2, SO THE NODE EXISTS ONLY WHERE CITATIONS ARE ENABLED. With the
      extension off, `[@key]: entry` is not a citation definition and it is not
      a reference definition either -- the leading `@` is reserved against that
      in core -- so the line is ordinary paragraph text. A default-profile parse
      therefore never produces this type, which is why the schema declares it
      and the core corpus cannot reach it.

      WHERE A DEFINITION IS RECOGNIZED IS NOT DECIDED HERE: this clause rules
      the node's shape, not recognition or hoisting from containers
      (CARVE-P12-029's link-reference hoisting and CARVE-P12-026's
      container-local abbreviation refusal). An emitted
      node satisfying this shape conforms whichever recognition policy applies.

   18a. A CITATION ITEM IS A POSITIONED INLINE NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-038].
      Every item in `citation_group.children` carries `type: "citation"` and the
      `pos` of PART 12 §4, spanning its optional prefix, `-` author
      suppression marker, `@key`, locator and suffix. Its existing `key`,
      `prefix`, `locator`, `locatorLabel`, `locatorValue`, `suffix`,
      `suppressAuthor`, `number` and `useIndex` fields are unchanged.

      The item is content, not bookkeeping: an editor navigates to a locator,
      a diagnostic points at one key among several, and an AST transform may
      replace one citation without replacing its group. It therefore follows
      the same typed, positioned node contract as every other content-bearing
      value in the tree and belongs to the inline profile vocabulary.

      BELONGING TO THAT VOCABULARY IS NOT THE SAME AS BEING AN INLINE CHILD:
      CARVE-P12-059 holds that rule, and the inline dispatch does not name `citation`.

      PART 12 §4 GOVERNS THE POSITION, AS IT DOES EVERYWHERE ELSE, exemptions included:
      a producer unable to place a node omits `pos`; synthesized nodes MUST
      omit it. The schema MUST NOT require `pos` on `citation`. A parser
      still places every citation it reads
      ([carve#2192](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2192)).

   19. TABLE COLUMNS CARRY AUTHORED AND RESOLVED ALIGNMENT -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-039]. A `table` MUST carry `columns` describing each column up
      to its widest row, including columns with no authored settings. An empty
      table carries an empty array. Each entry is a typed `table_column` node with
      optional authored `align`, `valign`, and fractional `width`, plus
      `resolvedAlign` after header-row precedence. The `authored` marker says
      an explicit column record existed. A generated column may omit it.

      A header cell with explicit alignment supplies the resolved default for
      the column it starts in, except when a span prevents that precedence.
      Authored alignment remains separate so `fmt` writes the source spelling.
      Widths need not sum to one; a sum above one is a diagnostic, not an
      invalid exchange tree. A consumer reads `resolvedAlign` rather than
      redoing header precedence.

      Source `aligns`, `valigns`, and `widths` lists still describe authored
      column settings. Each list is positional; an empty entry means unset.
      A cell's explicit setting wins over a column setting for the same axis.
      Authored `align` and `valign` on a cell are consumed as semantics and
      MUST NOT also appear as literal HTML `align` or `valign` attributes.

      SOURCE SPELLING. `aligns`, `valigns`, and `widths` are comma-separated
      lists. Empty entries are unset; source widths are percentages greater
      than zero and at most 100, divided by 100 in the AST. More entries than
      physical columns is an error. Fewer is valid but lints; a total width
      over 100 also lints. Per-field precedence is local: a cell marker wins,
      then an in-table column marker, then a table attribute. Competing
      column-level spellings lint and the in-table one wins.

      A ListTable cell marker may carry `align` and `valign`, each overriding
      the column setting for its own axis. A canonical writer keeps a native
      header marker where possible and uses table attributes for a covered
      column or a promoted plain header row. Widths emit percentages without
      trailing zeroes. HTML emits resolved cell alignment and a `colgroup` for
      widths. Markdown keeps horizontal alignment where its delimiter row can
      spell it; plain and ANSI ignore these fields. A target that cannot
      preserve the metadata reports its loss.

   20. TABLE ALIGNMENT RUNS CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT AXES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-040]. A cell
      alignment run is glued to its opening pipe, after an optional `=` kind
      marker, and is terminated by exactly one space after an optional glued
      attribute block. Horizontal markers are `<`, `~`, `>`; vertical markers
      are `^`, `~`, `v` for top, middle and bottom. A horizontal marker may
      stand alone; a vertical marker MUST follow a horizontal marker or the
      `?` inheritance placeholder. Vertical-then-horizontal order is invalid.
      `~~` means center plus middle. Any other
      duplicate axis is invalid and falls back visibly rather than consuming
      a prefix. The only valid order is horizontal (`x`) then vertical (`y`).

      A lone `~` is horizontal center. `?` is the horizontal-inheritance
      placeholder and is valid only before a vertical marker: `?^`, `?~` and
      `?v` contribute no cell-level `align`, so that field resolves from the
      column while the run contributes `top`, `middle` or `bottom` as the
      cell-level `valign`. A lone `?`, a reversed vertical-plus-`?` pair and
      every other `?` run are ordinary visible content. A native `|=` run
      supplies a default only from a head row (T9), and only for its starting
      physical column. A marker on
      a GFM-promoted head-row cell overrides the delimiter default only in
      that same column. Its colspan does not copy the default into covered
      columns. A plain `|` run supplies only that cell, including when
      `header-rows` later promotes the row to HTML headers. A run with no
      following space is not alignment: this deliberately changes the glued
      `|>text` family so marker runs have an unambiguous terminator. `lint`
      reports the old glued form and `fmt --migrate` inserts the space.

      `table_cell` carries optional `valign` beside `align`, with the same
      inheritance rule. A cell containing only `^` or `<`, with any space
      padding or none, remains a rowspan/colspan marker. The lone
      glyph takes precedence over an empty alignment run.

   21. U+0000 IS REPLACED ON INGEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-041]. A reader
      replaces every U+0000 with U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER in every string
      value it ingests. It does this BEFORE any other reading of the value -
      before a sentinel is looked for in it, before it is used as a key,
      before it reaches a renderer.

      THE SUBJECT IS THE DECODED VALUE, not the bytes of a JSON document. A
      string reaches an ingest by two doors and the rule is the same at both:
      decoded from JSON text, where the only spelling that can carry this
      character is the `\u0000` escape, and handed in directly by a host that
      built the tree in memory and called the ingest on it. This clause does
      NOT relax the JSON grammar: RFC 8259 forbids an unescaped U+0000 inside
      a string, so a raw byte in JSON text is a syntax error before any Carve
      rule is reached, and stays one. It is named here only because the second
      door has no JSON layer to raise it.

      THIS MIRRORS THE PARSE BOUNDARY, which is the whole of the rule: PART 0
      INPUT performs the same replacement on source, and PART 9 §29 carves
      U+0000 out of the content class on that basis. An AST is a second door
      into the same renderers; it takes the same doormat.

      A READER DOES NOT REFUSE THE VALUE.
      CARVE-P12-030 and CARVE-P12-031 refuse an unknown property and a deviant root, because those
      are structure a producer got wrong. This is different: the replacement is
      what the parse boundary already does to the identical string, so
      performing it is the documented reading rather than a repair. Refusing
      would make an ingested document stricter than the same document written
      as source.

      AN IMPORTER IS THE SAME BOUNDARY BY THE SAME REASONING, and is stated
      here as a SHOULD rather than a MUST because the format being imported may
      have its own rule.

   22. AN INGESTED VALUE THE SCHEMA CALLS ABSENT IS NORMALIZED AWAY --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-042]. `list.marker.start` is omitted when its value
      is 1. An encoder ingests `start: 1` on an ordered marker and drops it
      before publishing; the default and omission have the same meaning.
      Other start values remain, including zero and two. An encoder MUST NOT
      re-emit an ingested `start: 1`; retaining the default would publish a
      shape that differs from a parsed tree. The round-trip gate of PART 12 §6
      applies to parsed trees, which already use this form.

   23. A BLOCK IMAGE IS A NAMED FIELD ON THE PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-043]. A paragraph promoted by PART 9R R7 carries
      `blockImage: true`; an unpromoted one omits the field. The root
      `stages` array declares whether `blockImagePromotion` ran. A producer
      MUST NOT claim that stage ran unless the result on every paragraph is
      accurate. The flag is a resolution result, not a replacement for the
      authored paragraph or image.

      For example, a promoted paragraph remains a paragraph with an image
      child, `blockImage: true`, and the image's authored `key` and `rawRef`.
      The flag is set by the promotion phase, not inferred from a renderer's
      restriction on nested anchors. Keeping the paragraph type leaves
      existing consumers' node dispatch intact while making the resolution
      result explicit.

      AN INGESTED `blockImage` VALUE IS AUTHORITATIVE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-044]. A reader uses `true` when present. If the root declares
      `blockImagePromotion`, absence means not promoted. If it does not
      declare that stage, a reader may run promotion. A consumer MUST NOT
      rederive a result the producer already declared. Absence before that
      stage ran means the producer did not compute the result. A reader MUST
      NOT treat it as proof that the paragraph is ordinary, and MUST NOT
      reject a tree merely for omitting the field.

      `fmt` WRITES THE SOURCE SPELLING, NOT `blockImage` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-045]. `fmt` writes the SOURCE SPELLING,
      so promotion is not observable in `fmt` output and a document whose
      reference is never defined still round-trips to itself (PART 12 §6). The
      alternative makes the writer non-local: the same paragraph would be
      written differently depending on whether a definition appears somewhere
      else in the document.

      AN IMPORTER READS THE FIELD RATHER THAN GUESSING. An HTML importer that
      escapes a following `^ ` line because the image above it "would capture
      it" is deriving PART 9R R7 a third time, in a pipeline with no access to the
      phase (`docs/html-import.md`, the detached-caption case). It reads
      `blockImage` on the tree it built instead.

   24. A CHILDREN ARRAY HOLDS ONLY A CONTENT BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-046].
      Eleven `children` fields in `resources/ast-schema.json` hold a list of
      block nodes: on `document`, `admonition`, `block_quote`,
      `definition_description`, `directive`, `div`, `figure_group`, `footnote`,
      `line_block`, `list_item` and `section`. The types they admit are the
      CONTENT blocks. A CONTAINER-INTERNAL type is not among them.

      `table_row`, `table_cell`, `list_item`, `definition_term` and
      `definition_description` exist only inside the container that owns them.
      `table.children`, `table_row.children`, `list.children` and
      `definition_list.children` name each of them directly, so an unrelated `children`
      array holding one is a tree no parser produces and no renderer has a
      case for.

      THE UNION IS THE ENFORCEMENT. CARVE-P12-031(d) validates a payload against the
      schema, so the block union names content blocks only, and a `children`
      array holding a container-internal type fails at decode
      ([carve#2189](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2189)).

      A `list_item` REACHES ITS OWN CHILDREN THE SAME WAY. It holds content
      blocks, so a nested `list` is a child of an item and a nested
      `list_item` is not -- which is what the source already says.

   25. A REFERENCE NODE CARRIES A KEY AND NAMESPACE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-047]. A reference use or definition carries `key` and
      `namespace` together. The namespace distinguishes link, footnote,
      citation, abbreviation, and heading keys. A direct link or image with
      no reference key carries neither. `attrs.id` is an authored anchor and
      MUST NOT be used as a substitute for `key`.

      A reference use keeps the authored key even after resolution. `href`,
      `src`, expansion, and numbering are optional resolution results. An
      absent `href` or `src` on a reference use means unresolved; an explicit
      empty string means an empty destination. An absent expansion or number
      means no result was supplied. A producer MUST NOT emit `resolved`.

      For duplicate `attrs.id` values, the first node in document order wins.
      The producer reports a diagnostic for each later occurrence. A reader
      MUST NOT silently choose the last occurrence.

   26. A SPANNING CELL PUBLISHES ITS RESOLVED EXTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-048].
      A `table_cell` that the span walk of CARVE-P9-002 extended carries
      `colspan`, `rowspan`, or both: the number of columns and rows the cell
      occupies once every `<` and `^` marker that reached it has been consumed.
      They sit on the ORIGIN cell. Absent means 1, and an ingested 1 is
      normalized away on the reasoning CARVE-P12-042 gives for a list's `start`.

      THE MARKERS STAY. A continuation cell keeps its `span`, because that is
      what the author wrote and CARVE-P12-005 records the document rather than a
      rendering of it. The counts are ADDED ALONGSIDE, the same rule that lets
      a resolved reference keep `key` and `rawRef` beside `href` and a resolved
      footnote reference keep its key beside its number (PART 12 §5).

      A MARKER THAT FOUND NO ORIGIN CONTRIBUTES NOTHING. The walk is a total
      function: a `^` in the first row, a `<` in the first column, or a marker
      every intermediate position of which is consumed extends nobody, so no
      cell's count moves. The marker is still published, still carrying
      `span`. With no joined text it renders as an empty cell. A marker that
      carries joined `+` text (PART 9 §5 T6) keeps `span`, holds that text as
      its children, and renders a cell holding that text; a Carve writer
      writes the marker back on the standard row and the text on its `+` line
      ([carve#3158](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/3158)).

        | ^ | b |
        + t | u |

        table_row{ continuationLines: 1, children: [
          table_cell{ span: "rowspan" }[ cell_paragraph[ "t" ] ],
          table_cell[ cell_paragraph[ "b", cell_wrap{line:1}, "u" ] ] ] }

      WHY IT IS PUBLISHED AT ALL: PART 12 §5 publishes resolution results alongside
      authored constructs so consumers need not reimplement the resolution
      rules, including this span walk's orphan and blocked cases.

      THE COUNT IS NOT A SECOND SPELLING OF THE MARKERS. A payload whose
      counts contradict its markers is invalid, in the sense CARVE-P12-030 means: the
      walk is a function of the markers, so the two cannot disagree in a tree
      any producer built. A reader is not required to re-run the walk to check.

   27. A TABLE CELL ALWAYS CARRIES BLOCK CHILDREN -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-049]. `table_cell.children` is a block list, including for
      cells spelled with inline pipe content. Such a cell holds one
      `cell_paragraph` whose inline `children` may contain `cell_wrap`.
      `cell_wrap` is invalid in any other inline context. A cell imported
      with block content may hold ordinary block nodes instead. The old
      `table_cell.blocks` alternative is removed.
      The fallback matrix row `table_cell.children` records target behavior.
      An unspellable block boundary is reported as `field-unspellable` for
      `table_cell.children` through the CARVE-P11-046 channel. The old
      `table_cell.blocks` field is mapped to that slot before conversion.

      A tight cell renders one `cell_paragraph` without a paragraph wrapper
      in HTML and writes its inline content as a pipe cell. Other block
      content uses ordinary block rendering inside the cell. Markdown, plain,
      and ANSI targets flatten block cells to ONE LINE in document order,
      separating contributing blocks with one space. A canonical writer
      reports lost block boundaries and unspellable structures; it does not
      drop them without a conversion diagnostic (CARVE-P11-003).


        table_cell{ children: [ paragraph "one", paragraph "two" ] }
          -> | one two |

      The report is once per flattened cell: the writer dissolved a block
      boundary. It is a conversion diagnostic, not a render loss. A
      source-spelled pipe cell uses `cell_paragraph`, rather than an ordinary
      paragraph, so the canonical source wrapper has only one representation
      ([carve#2191](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2191)).

      A code block contributes its literal value, neither its header nor its label, which are not payload.
      A line block contributes its lines, a newline between two lines becoming one space and a trailing newline nothing.
      A raw block and an abbreviation definition contribute NOTHING to a flattened cell.

      Span markers and resolved extents remain on the cell, independent of
      the content slot (CARVE-P12-048). The cell's `sourceContent` may record
      whether the source was inline or blocks, but it does not create a
      second content slot.

   28. SMALL CAPS SURVIVE INTERCHANGE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-050]. A
      `small_caps` inline node carries `children`, plus optional `attrs` and
      `pos` under the ordinary inline-node rules. It records a semantic
      small-caps distinction supplied by an AST consumer or format bridge.

      The wrapper is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066). Structured exporters
      MAY map it to equivalent small caps. The fallback matrix row `small_caps`
      gives HTML, Markdown (CARVE-P11-045), plain and ANSI output.
      HTML renders `<span class="smallcaps">`, merging `attrs` under ordinary
      span rules. Plain and ANSI render the children without changing letter
      case. The canonical writer MUST write the children without the small-caps
      wrapper. It MUST preserve any `attrs` on an ordinary attributed span
      around those children. It MUST NOT discard children or change their
      case. It reports `structure-unspellable` for `small_caps` on
      the CARVE-P11-046 channel.
      A source spelling can be specified separately without changing this node.

   29. A DISPLAY EQUATION MAY CARRY A PREFIX AND NUMBER -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-051]. A `math` node MAY carry `numberPrefix`, a non-empty
      authored string with no surrounding whitespace, used as its visible
      numbering prefix and counter bucket. The cross-reference identifier is
      `attrs.id`. A resolved labeled display equation MAY also carry `number`,
      a positive integer assigned by PART 9R R5a. Only `display: true` may
      carry a number, and a number requires `numberPrefix`.

      A numbered enclosing figure or figure group owns the number of its math
      descendants, so those descendants retain `numberPrefix` without their
      own `number`. `figure.children` replaces the old single target. A
      canonical writer reports `field-unspellable` for `math.numberPrefix`
      or `math.number` through the CARVE-P11-046 channel rather than silently
      dropping it. The former prefix field was `math.label`.

   ============================================================================ *)
